---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const jsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "HowTo",
  "name": "How to Contribute to Open Source on GitHub",
  "description": "A step-by-step walkthrough of the full contribution workflow — fork, branch, write code, commit, and open your first pull request.",
  "step": [
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "position": 1, "name": "Fork and Clone the Repository", "text": "Click the Fork button on GitHub to create a copy on your account, then clone it locally: git clone https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/repository-name.git" },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "position": 2, "name": "Keep Your Fork Synced", "text": "Add the original repo as upstream: git remote add upstream URL, then git fetch upstream and git merge upstream/main." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "position": 3, "name": "Create a Feature Branch", "text": "Never work on main. Create a branch: git checkout -b fix-login-button" },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "position": 4, "name": "Write Code and Commit", "text": "Make your changes, test them, then commit: git add . && git commit -m 'fix: updated login button'" },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "position": 5, "name": "Push Your Branch", "text": "Push your branch to your fork: git push origin fix-login-button" },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "position": 6, "name": "Open a Pull Request", "text": "On the original repository, click Compare & pull request. Fill out the template explaining what changed and why. Link the issue with Fixes #123." },
    { "@type": "HowToStep", "position": 7, "name": "Respond to Review", "text": "The maintainer will review your code and may request changes. Make updates locally, commit, and push — the PR updates automatically." }
  ]
});
---

<Layout title="How to Contribute to Open Source on GitHub | Repo Wave" description="A step-by-step walkthrough of the full contribution workflow — fork, branch, write code, commit, and open your first pull request." jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16">
    <a href="/guide" class="inline-flex items-center text-sm font-medium text-indigo-600 hover:text-indigo-800 mb-8 transition-colors">
      <svg class="w-4 h-4 mr-1" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 19l-7-7m0 0l7-7m-7 7h18"></path></svg>
      Back to Guides
    </a>

    <header class="mb-10">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-4 text-sm font-medium text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">
        <span class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800 px-2 py-1 rounded text-indigo-700 dark:text-indigo-400">Part 2</span>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>8 min read</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 dark:text-gray-100 tracking-tight mb-6">GitHub Contribution Guide</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">The step-by-step workflow for getting your code from your laptop into an open-source project.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose prose-lg prose-indigo dark:prose-invert text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 max-w-none">
      
      <h2>1. The Fork and Clone</h2>
      <p>
        You cannot push code directly to someone else's project. Instead, you create a copy (a fork) on your GitHub account.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Click the <strong>Fork</strong> button in the top right corner of the repository on GitHub.</li>
        <li>Copy the URL of <em>your</em> fork.</li>
        <li>Open your terminal and clone it to your computer:</li>
      </ul>
      <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/repository-name.git
cd repository-name</code></pre>

      <h2>2. Keep Your Fork Synced</h2>
      <p>
        Before you start coding, you need to connect your local copy to the original project (the "upstream") so you can pull the latest changes.
      </p>
      <pre><code>git remote add upstream https://github.com/ORIGINAL-OWNER/repository-name.git
git fetch upstream
git checkout main
git merge upstream/main</code></pre>

      <h2>3. Create a Branch</h2>
      <p>
        Never make changes on the <code>main</code> branch. Always create a new branch named after the feature or issue.
      </p>
      <pre><code>git checkout -b fix-login-button</code></pre>

      <h2>4. Write Code and Commit</h2>
      <p>
        Make your changes in your code editor. Test them to make sure nothing is broken. Then, commit your changes.
      </p>
      <pre><code>git add .
git commit -m "fix: updated login button color to blue"</code></pre>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 dark:bg-blue-900/20 border-l-4 border-blue-500 dark:border-blue-600 p-4 my-6 rounded-r-lg">
        <p class="text-blue-800 dark:text-blue-300 font-medium m-0"><strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Use clear, descriptive commit messages. Many projects use <a href="https://www.conventionalcommits.org/" target="_blank">Conventional Commits</a> (e.g., <code>fix:</code>, <code>feat:</code>, <code>docs:</code>).</p>
      </div>

      <h2>5. Push Your Changes</h2>
      <p>
        Push your new branch up to <em>your</em> fork on GitHub.
      </p>
      <pre><code>git push origin fix-login-button</code></pre>

      <h2>6. Open a Pull Request (PR)</h2>
      <p>
        Go to the original repository on GitHub. You should see a green button that says <strong>Compare & pull request</strong>. Click it!
      </p>
      <p>Fill out the PR template carefully:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Explain <em>why</em> you are making the change.</li>
        <li>Explain <em>how</em> you tested it.</li>
        <li>Link the original issue by writing <code>Fixes #123</code> (replace 123 with the issue number). This automatically closes the issue when your PR is merged.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>7. Review and Celebrate</h2>
      <p>
        The maintainer will review your code. They might ask for changes. Don't take it personally! Just make the changes locally, commit, and push again. The PR updates automatically. Curious about <a href="/guide/how-maintainers-review-prs" class="text-indigo-600 dark:text-indigo-400 hover:underline">what maintainers are actually looking for when they open your PR</a>? We have a guide for that too.
      </p>

    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
